export const F6={title:'Primary care, prevention and health equity (Objective d)',hook:'Where you live, what you earn and whether you can get to a clinic shape your health as much as your genes. Family physicians see these forces every day—and objective (d) asks how primary care and prevention help every community reach its best possible health.',objectives:['Explain why primary care and preventive medicine improve population health.','Describe levels of prevention and evidence-based screening.','Define health equity, health disparities and social determinants of health.','Describe how family physicians advocate for patients and communities.'],sections:[
['Why primary care matters',[
'Primary care is the **front door** of the health system and the place where most health problems are prevented, detected early and managed. Studies across countries and U.S. regions show that a strong primary care workforce is associated with **lower mortality**, fewer avoidable hospitalizations and emergency visits, better management of chronic disease and **lower overall costs**. **Continuity**—seeing the same physician over time—is linked to better outcomes and patient trust. Because family physicians care for whole families, they can spot patterns such as shared risk factors, household stress or barriers to care that would be missed in fragmented specialty visits.',
flip('What primary care does for communities',[['Prevention','Vaccines, screenings and counseling stop disease early'],['Early detection','Catches cancer, diabetes and hypertension sooner'],['Chronic disease management','Keeps conditions controlled and patients out of the hospital'],['Coordination','Prevents duplicated tests and missed follow-up'],['Continuity','Builds trust and improves adherence'],['Lower cost','Fewer avoidable emergency visits and admissions']])]],
['Levels of prevention',[
'**Primary prevention** stops disease before it starts—immunizations, healthy eating, physical activity, tobacco cessation and seat belts. **Secondary prevention** detects disease early, before symptoms, through **screening**: blood pressure, cholesterol, diabetes, cervical, breast and colorectal cancer screening and depression screening. **Tertiary prevention** reduces complications of established disease—cardiac rehabilitation, diabetic foot and eye exams and medication management after a stroke. Family physicians follow evidence-based recommendations from the **U.S. Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF)**, which grades screenings and preventive services by the strength of evidence, and the CDC’s immunization schedules.',
sort('Level of prevention',['Primary','Secondary','Tertiary'],[['Childhood vaccines',0],['Counseling to quit smoking',0],['Colorectal cancer screening',1],['Blood pressure screening',1],['Cardiac rehabilitation after a heart attack',2],['Annual diabetic eye exam for a person with diabetes',2]])]],
['Health equity and its determinants',[
'**Health equity** means everyone has a fair and just opportunity to attain their highest level of health. **Health disparities** are preventable differences in disease, injury or access experienced by socially disadvantaged populations. Many differences come from **social determinants of health (SDOH)**—the conditions in which people are born, live, learn, work, play and age. **Healthy People 2030** groups them into five domains: **economic stability**, **education access and quality**, **health care access and quality**, **neighborhood and built environment** and **social and community context**. Examples include income, housing, food security, transportation, safe neighborhoods, discrimination and social support. Clinical care alone accounts for only part of health outcomes; addressing social needs is essential.',
match('Match the example to its SDOH domain',[['A family cannot afford rent and food','Economic stability'],['Limited reading level affects understanding instructions','Education access and quality'],['No clinic within 40 miles','Health care access and quality'],['No sidewalks or safe parks','Neighborhood and built environment'],['Social isolation of an older adult','Social and community context'],['Lack of health insurance','Health care access and quality']])]],
['How family physicians advance equity',[
'Family physicians address equity in the exam room and beyond. They **screen for social needs** (food, housing, transportation, safety) and connect patients with community resources; use **interpreters** and plain language; offer **culturally responsive** care that respects beliefs and preferences; provide care in **federally qualified health centers**, rural clinics and tribal health systems; extend access through **evening hours, telehealth and school-based clinics**; and build trust in communities that have experienced discrimination. Beyond the clinic, they **advocate**—for vaccine access, insurance coverage, safe housing, tobacco control and healthy food—by serving on boards, testifying to legislators, partnering with public health departments and conducting community research. The AAFP and state chapters organize advocacy on behalf of patients and the specialty.',
kase('Beyond the prescription',{a:{text:'A patient’s diabetes is poorly controlled. She says she skips insulin at the end of the month and often eats whatever is cheapest. What should the family physician consider first?',choices:[['Screen for food and financial insecurity and connect her with resources and lower-cost medication','b','Correct. Social needs are driving the poor control.',true],['Increase her insulin dose','b','A higher dose does not solve skipped doses and food insecurity.',false]]},b:{text:'Many patients at the clinic report the same problem. What advocacy step can the physician take?',choices:[['Partner with a food bank to start a clinic food pantry and advocate for insulin cost protections','c','Correct. Advocacy addresses root causes for many patients.',true],['Nothing—it is outside medicine','c','Family physicians advocate for community health.',false]]},c:{text:'The clinic’s food pantry and patient assistance program help many patients improve their glucose control.',end:true}})]],
['Presenting objective (d) powerfully',[
'This topic is easiest to present through **stories and examples**: a patient whose health improved when a social need was met, a rural community that gained a clinic, or a screening program that caught cancer early. Ask your interviewee for a de-identified example of advocacy or equity work—without names or identifying details, consistent with **HIPAA**. Explain clearly how the family physician’s role makes the difference, since the rubric’s top band requires it to be “explicitly clear how the Family Medicine Physician plays a vital role.” A simple visual—such as a diagram showing that clinical care is only one of many influences on health—can make the idea memorable.',
chk('What must be explicit to earn full points on objective (d)?',['How the family physician plays a vital role in primary care, prevention and health equity','The physician’s home address','A list of every disease','The cost of the competitor’s visual aids'],'Connect the physician’s role directly to equity outcomes.')]],
["Measuring impact: quality and population health",["Family physicians track whether care is working through **quality measures**: the percentage of patients with hypertension whose blood pressure is controlled, patients with diabetes whose A1c is in target, children who are fully immunized, adults up to date on cancer screening and patients screened for depression and social needs. **Population health management** uses registries in the electronic health record to identify patients who are overdue for care and reach out to them, rather than waiting for them to come in. Comparing results by race, ethnicity, language, insurance and neighborhood reveals **disparities** so the team can target improvements—a direct, measurable way family physicians advance health equity.","Practices recognized as **patient-centered medical homes** organize around these goals with team-based care, expanded access and care coordination. Hospitals and health departments also conduct **community health needs assessments**, and family physicians often help interpret the results and design programs. If your interviewee’s clinic improved a measure—for example, raising colorectal screening rates through mailed home tests—it can become a memorable example in your presentation.",{"t":"example","title":"Finding a disparity in clinic data","prompt":"A clinic’s registry shows that 72% of English-speaking patients with hypertension have controlled blood pressure, but only 54% of Spanish-speaking patients do. What might the team do?","steps":["Recognize the 18-point gap as a disparity worth addressing.","Ask patients about barriers such as interpreter access, medication cost or appointment times.","Add bilingual education, interpreter-supported visits and community health workers.","Re-measure control rates for both groups after several months."],"answer":"The team targets the barriers driving the gap and tracks both groups until the disparity narrows—an example of using data to advance health equity. (The numbers are an invented illustration.)"}]]
],takeaways:['Strong primary care is linked to lower mortality, fewer avoidable hospital visits and lower costs; continuity builds trust.','Primary prevention stops disease, secondary detects it early (USPSTF screening), tertiary limits complications.','Health equity requires addressing SDOH—economic stability, education, health care access, neighborhood and social context—through care and advocacy.'],terms:[['Health equity','Fair opportunity for everyone to attain their highest level of health.'],['Health disparity','Preventable difference in health affecting disadvantaged groups.'],['Social determinants of health','Conditions where people live, learn, work and age that affect health.']],mcqs:[q('Colorectal cancer screening is an example of:','Secondary prevention','Primary prevention','Tertiary prevention','Treatment only','Screening detects disease early.'),q('Which organization grades evidence for preventive services?','USPSTF','NFL','FDA Food Division','HOSA','The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force.'),q('Which is a social determinant of health?','Access to safe housing','Eye color','Blood type','Height','SDOH are social and economic conditions.'),q('Health equity means:','Everyone has a fair opportunity to attain their highest level of health','Everyone receives identical treatment regardless of need','Only insured people receive care','Health depends only on genetics','Equity addresses unfair differences.'),q('Childhood immunizations are:','Primary prevention','Secondary prevention','Tertiary prevention','Palliative care','They prevent disease before it starts.'),q('Strong primary care is associated with:','Lower mortality and lower costs','Higher emergency use','Worse chronic disease control','More duplicated tests','Evidence supports primary care.'),q('Which action is advocacy?','Testifying to legislators about insulin costs','Ignoring social needs','Refusing interpreters','Closing evening hours','Advocacy addresses root causes.'),q('Healthy People 2030 groups SDOH into how many domains?','Five','Two','Ten','Twenty','Economic, education, health care, neighborhood and social context.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how family physicians use primary care and preventive medicine to reduce health disparities, giving examples of each level of prevention and at least two social determinants of health.',rubric:[['Primary care impact',['primary care','continuity','mortality','cost','access']],['Levels of prevention',['primary','secondary','tertiary','screening','vaccin','uspstf']],['Social determinants and equity',['social determinant','housing','food','transportation','equity','disparit']],['Family physician role and advocacy',['screen','resource','interpreter','advocate','fqhc','community']]],model:'Family physicians are the first contact for most health needs, and communities with strong primary care have lower mortality, fewer avoidable emergency visits and lower costs because problems are prevented and managed early within a trusting, continuous relationship. They practice primary prevention through vaccines and counseling on tobacco, diet and activity; secondary prevention through USPSTF-recommended screenings for blood pressure, diabetes, depression and breast, cervical and colorectal cancer; and tertiary prevention by managing chronic disease to prevent complications, such as diabetic eye and foot exams. Health disparities often come from social determinants of health—economic stability, education, health care access, neighborhood conditions and social context—such as food insecurity, unstable housing or lack of transportation. Family physicians screen for these needs, connect patients with community resources, use interpreters and culturally responsive care, work in FQHCs and rural clinics and offer telehealth and extended hours. They also advocate for insurance coverage, affordable medications and healthy communities, making their role vital to achieving health equity.'},sources:[RULES,USPSTF,HP2030,CDC_EQUITY]};

export const F7={title:'Planning and conducting the interviews',hook:'Your two interviews are the raw material for the most valuable parts of the presentation: the physician’s story and your own reflection. Who you interview, how you prepare and what you ask determine whether you leave with facts anyone could find—or a story that moves an audience.',objectives:['Identify eligible interviewees and use AAFP and local connections to reach them.','Complete the Interview Verification Form correctly.','Write questions mapped to each learning objective, especially the WHY.','Conduct interviews professionally and ethically, including virtual formats.'],sections:[
['Who you may interview',[
'Competitors conduct **at least two interviews with two separate people**. **One must be a family medicine physician**; a **retired** family physician is acceptable. The second may be a **medical student interested in family medicine** or **another family medicine physician**. **Both may not be medical students**. You may conduct more than two interviews. Interview length and format—in person, video call or telephone—are up to you and the interviewee. If you lack connections, complete the **AAFP online form** to be matched with a physician and a medical student, ideally **four or more weeks** before you need the interviews. You may also use your HOSA advisor, health science teacher, family, friends or community partners to find interviewees.',
sort('Is this pair of interviewees valid?',['Valid pair','Not valid'],[['Family physician + medical student interested in family medicine',0],['Two family physicians',0],['Retired family physician + medical student',0],['Two medical students',1],['Medical student + nurse practitioner',1],['Cardiologist + medical student',1]])]],
['The Interview Verification Form',[
'For each interview, the form records the **name and title of the interviewee**, **date(s) of interview**, **practice, clinic or hospital name and address**, the interviewee’s **email address** and their **signature**, with a checkbox if they do not wish to be contacted by the AAFP. Make extra copies if you interview more than two people. Bring the form to the interview or send it securely in advance, explain why it is needed and thank the interviewee for supporting the event. Upload the completed forms as **one PDF** by **May 15**; missing the upload costs **15 points**.',
chk('Which detail is NOT on the Interview Verification Form?',['The interviewee’s salary','Name and title','Practice address','Signature'],'Salary is never requested on the form.')]],
['Writing questions for each objective',[
'Build an interview guide organized by the six objectives, with open-ended questions and follow-ups. Factual topics (a–c) can be confirmed through research, so use interview time to gather **perspective and examples**. Objective (e) needs the most care: the guidelines emphasize asking questions that **elicit emotion** and uncover **why** the physician chose family medicine.',
{t:'table',caption:'A sample interview guide',head:['Objective','Sample questions'],rows:[['a. Demand','How long do new patients wait to see you? How has demand changed in your community?'],['b. Education and options','What surprised you in residency? Why did you choose your setting or emphasis?'],['c. Work, lifestyle, finances','What does a typical week look like? How do you protect time for family? How did you manage loans?'],['d. Equity and prevention','Can you share (without identifying anyone) a time you helped a patient overcome a barrier to care?'],['e. The WHY','What moment made you choose family medicine? What keeps you going on hard days?'],['f. Fit for me','What qualities make someone thrive in family medicine? What advice would you give me?']]},
match('Match the question to its objective',[['“What moment made you choose family medicine?”','e. The WHY'],['“How do you manage work-life balance?”','c. Work and lifestyle'],['“How has your patient wait list changed?”','a. Demand'],['“Why did you pursue a sports medicine fellowship?”','b. Career options'],['“How do you address patients’ social needs?”','d. Health equity'],['“What traits help students succeed in this field?”','f. Is it right for me?']])]],
['Conducting the interview',[
'Contact the interviewee professionally, explain HOSA and the event and propose times. Confirm the format and the date the day before. Dress professionally, arrive or log in early and test video and audio for virtual interviews. **Ask permission** before recording; if declined, take careful notes. Begin with rapport (“How did your morning clinic go?”), move from factual to personal questions, **listen actively** and follow up on emotional moments (“What did that feel like?” “What happened next?”). Respect privacy—never ask for patient names, and remove identifying details from stories. Close by thanking them, asking for the signed verification form if not already completed and asking whether they would like to see the final presentation. Send a thank-you note within a day or two.',
order('Interview flow',['Introduce yourself, HOSA and the purpose','Ask permission to record','Build rapport with an easy opening question','Ask factual questions on objectives a–d','Move to the personal story and WHY','Follow up on emotional moments','Ask advice for students like you','Thank them and confirm the verification form'])]],
['Interviewing the medical student',[
'A medical student interested in family medicine offers a different view: the recent experience of applying to medical school, the cost and how they are managing it, what clinical rotations are like, why they are drawn to family medicine and what their classmates think about primary care. Their perspective can help peers picture themselves on the path. Ask how they prepared in high school and college, what they wish they had known and how HOSA or similar experiences helped. Comparing the student’s and physician’s answers can reveal how the career looks at different stages—a useful contrast for your presentation.',
kase('A short answer',{a:{text:'You ask the physician why she chose family medicine. She says, “I liked the variety.” What should you do next?',choices:[['Ask a follow-up such as “Was there a patient or moment when you knew this was your path?”','b','Correct. Follow-ups uncover the story behind the answer.',true],['Move to the next question on salary','b','You would miss the WHY, worth 20 points.',false]]},b:{text:'She describes caring for a family through a grandmother’s death and a grandchild’s birth in the same month. What do you note?',choices:[['Her words and emotions, with the family’s details left out to protect privacy','c','Correct. Capture the emotion while protecting privacy.',true],['The family’s names and address','c','Never record identifying patient details.',false]]},c:{text:'You now have a powerful, privacy-safe story that shows why she loves continuity of care.',end:true}})]],
["Turning interview notes into presentation content",["Right after each interview, expand your notes while details are fresh. If you recorded with permission, review the recording and mark the strongest moments with time stamps. Then **sort the material by objective** (a–f), highlighting short, vivid quotes that capture emotion or insight. Verify any facts the interviewee mentioned—such as program names or loan amounts—against authoritative sources before putting them on a slide. Compare the physician’s and medical student’s answers to find contrasts or common threads that can structure your story.","Choose quotes carefully: one or two sentences in the interviewee’s exact words are more powerful than long paraphrases. Ask yourself, “Does this quote move the story forward or help peers understand an objective?” If you plan to use a photo of the interviewee, ask permission. Finally, send a thank-you note and, if they wish, share the finished presentation—many physicians appreciate seeing how their story inspired students.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Keep for the presentation or leave out?","buckets":["Keep","Leave out"],"items":[["A short exact quote about why they chose the career",0],["A de-identified story about helping a patient access care",0],["A patient’s full name and diagnosis",1],["An unverified statistic the interviewee guessed",1],["A contrast between the physician’s and student’s journeys",0],["A detail the physician asked you not to share",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Interview at least two separate people: one family physician (retired is acceptable) and a medical student interested in family medicine or another family physician—never two students.','The signed Interview Verification Form lists name, title, dates, practice address and email; upload as one PDF by May 15.','Map questions to all six objectives, ask permission to record, follow up on emotional moments and protect patient privacy.'],terms:[['Interview guide','A planned set of questions organized by topic.'],['Follow-up question','A question that builds on an answer to gain depth.'],['Rapport','A comfortable, trusting connection with the interviewee.']],mcqs:[q('Which interview pair is NOT allowed?','Two medical students','Family physician and medical student','Two family physicians','Retired family physician and medical student','At least one must be a family physician.'),q('Is a retired family medicine physician acceptable?','Yes','No','Only if they still see patients','Only with AAFP approval','The guidelines allow it.'),q('If you lack connections, you can:','Complete the AAFP online form to be matched','Interview a random specialist','Skip the interviews','Use online articles instead','AAFP connects competitors.'),q('Which objective needs questions that elicit emotion?','e. The WHY','a. Demand','b. Education','c. Finances','The story drives the presentation.'),q('Before recording an interview, you must:','Ask permission','Record secretly','Post it online','Share it with peers first','Respect consent.'),q('Which belongs on the Interview Verification Form?','Practice, clinic or hospital name and address','The physician’s salary','Patient names','The competitor’s GPA','It verifies the interview.'),q('Which question best uncovers the WHY?','“Was there a moment you knew this was your path?”','“What is the median salary?”','“How many years is residency?”','“What is your clinic’s address?”','Open, personal questions reveal motivation.'),q('Interview formats may include:','In person, video call or telephone','Only in person','Only email','Only text messages','Format is flexible.')],frq:{prompt:'Design an interview plan for the Family Medicine Physician event: choose your two interviewees, write one question for each learning objective, and explain how you will conduct the interviews professionally and ethically.',rubric:[['Eligible interviewees',['family physician','medical student','retired','aafp','two']],['Questions for objectives',['demand','education','lifestyle','equity','why','right for me']],['Professional conduct',['permission','record','rapport','follow-up','thank']],['Ethics and verification',['privacy','hipaa','identifying','verification form','signature','may 15']]],model:'I would interview a practicing family physician from my community and a medical student interested in family medicine, using the AAFP connection form four or more weeks ahead if I could not find them through my advisor or family. My questions: (a) How long do new patients wait, and how has demand changed? (b) Why did you choose your residency, setting or fellowship? (c) What does a typical week look like, and how do you balance work, family and loans? (d) Without identifying anyone, can you share a time you helped a patient overcome a barrier to care? (e) Was there a moment or patient that made you choose family medicine, and what keeps you going on hard days? (f) What qualities help someone thrive in family medicine? I would contact them professionally, confirm the format, ask permission before recording, build rapport, listen actively and follow up on emotional moments. I would never record patient names or identifying details, would have each interviewee complete and sign the Interview Verification Form, upload it as one PDF before May 15 and send thank-you notes.'},sources:[RULES,AAFP,HIPAA]};

export const F8={title:'Telling the WHY and answering “Is it right for me?”',hook:'People don’t remember lists of facts; they remember stories. Objective (e)—the story of why your physician chose family medicine—and objective (f)—whether the career fits you—are worth 40 points together. They are where your presentation earns its heart.',objectives:['Apply the “Start With Why” framework to the physician’s story.','Structure a story with a clear arc, vivid detail and emotional impact.','Tell the story ethically and accurately.','Write an honest, specific reflection on whether family medicine is right for you.'],sections:[
['Start With Why',[
'HOSA recommends Simon Sinek’s talk *Start With Why*. Sinek describes a **Golden Circle** with three rings: **What** (what someone does), **How** (how they do it) and, at the center, **Why** (their purpose or belief). Most people communicate from the outside in—what, then how—while inspiring communicators start with **why**, which connects to emotion and decision-making. For your presentation, the **what** is the physician’s work (caring for patients of all ages), the **how** is the way they practice (continuity, prevention, community partnerships) and the **why** is the purpose that drives them (for example, “no family in my hometown should have to drive two hours to see a doctor”).',
match('Match the statement to the Golden Circle',[['“I care for patients of all ages in a rural clinic.”','What'],['“I build long relationships and see whole families.”','How'],['“I believe every family deserves a doctor who knows them.”','Why'],['“I deliver babies and manage chronic disease.”','What'],['“I partner with schools and the health department.”','How'],['“I want to give back to the town that raised me.”','Why']])]],
['Building the story arc',[
'A compelling story has a **beginning** (who the physician is and where they started), a **turning point** (a moment, person or challenge that revealed their purpose), **struggle** (obstacles—cost, doubt, long training, a difficult patient outcome) and **resolution** (who they are now and why it matters). Use **concrete, sensory details** and the physician’s **own words** in short quotes. Show emotion through specific moments rather than adjectives: “She still keeps a drawing a six-year-old patient made the day his asthma attacks stopped” is more powerful than “She is very caring.” End with a **call to action**—the rubric’s top band describes a story that “evokes emotion, is highly impactful and encourages a call to action.”',
order('Arrange the story arc',['Introduce the physician and their background','Describe the turning point that revealed their purpose','Share the obstacles they faced','Show who they are now and the impact on patients','Connect their WHY to the audience','Close with a call to action']),
flip('Telling vs showing',[['“Dr. Ruiz is passionate.”','“Dr. Ruiz still drives to a patient’s home every Thursday because he cannot get to clinic.”'],['“She loves her community.”','“She returned to the town where she was the first in her family to graduate high school.”'],['“He works hard.”','“He finished his charts at midnight after delivering twins, then made it to his daughter’s recital.”']])]],
['Telling the story ethically',[
'A story about a real person must be **accurate and respectful**. Confirm details you are unsure about, quote exactly, and ask the physician whether there is anything they would prefer you not share publicly. Any patient stories must be **de-identified**—no names, dates or details that could identify someone—consistent with **HIPAA** expectations. Do not exaggerate for effect; authentic stories are more persuasive. If you interviewed two physicians, you may use one or both stories, and the medical student’s journey can support the narrative.',
kase('Using a sensitive detail',{a:{text:'Your physician shared that her own mother’s illness inspired her career and became emotional. She said it was fine to mention. How should you present it?',choices:[['Share it respectfully in her words, focusing on how it shaped her purpose','b','Correct. She consented, and the detail explains her WHY.',true],['Add dramatic details she did not mention','b','Never embellish a real person’s story.',false]]},b:{text:'She also described a patient’s overdose in detail, including the patient’s street. What do you include?',choices:[['Only a de-identified version, such as “a young patient struggling with addiction”','c','Correct. Remove identifying details.',true],['The full details for impact','c','That would violate patient privacy.',false]]},c:{text:'Your story is honest, moving and respectful of everyone in it.',end:true}})]],
['Is family medicine right for me?',[
'Objective (f) is about **you**. Judges award 20 points for explaining “with passion and in detail” why family medicine would—or would **not**—be a good fit. HOSA explicitly allows either conclusion. Build your reflection from specific evidence: what you learned in the interviews that intrigued you, how the career matches your strengths, values and preferences, what concerns you (such as length of training or documentation) and how you would address them. Connect to concrete experiences—volunteering, HOSA events, a family health experience—and end with your next step, such as shadowing a family physician or exploring a pipeline program.',
sort('Strong or weak reflection?',['Strong and specific','Weak or vague'],[['“Hearing Dr. Lee describe caring for three generations made me realize I want long-term relationships, like the ones I built coaching youth soccer.”',0],['“It seems cool.”',1],['“I learned that I’d rather focus on one organ system, so I’m now exploring cardiology—but I value primary care’s role.”',0],['“My parents want me to be a doctor.”',1],['“The NHSC scholarship makes the cost feel manageable, so I plan to research it.”',0],['“I don’t know.”',1]])]],
['Weaving story and facts together',[
'The guidelines invite you to organize content in any manner, “potentially weaving facts and data to the story.” Instead of presenting objectives a–d as separate lectures, you can use the physician’s journey as a **thread**: their medical school and residency illustrate objective (b), their typical week and loan repayment illustrate (c), their rural practice and wait list illustrate (a) and (d), and the turning point is (e). Your reflection (f) then answers the question the story raises for the audience. This structure keeps the audience engaged and makes the presentation distinctive—worth 15 points on its own.',
chk('Why weave the learning objectives into the physician’s story?',['It keeps the audience engaged and makes the presentation distinctive while covering every objective','It lets you skip objectives','It shortens the presentation to two minutes','It avoids citing sources'],'Integrated storytelling covers all objectives memorably.')]],
["A model WHY segment",["Seeing a model helps. The example below shows about ninety seconds of a WHY segment built from an invented interview. Notice how it starts with purpose, uses a turning point and concrete detail, includes a short quote and ends by turning to the audience.",{"t":"example","title":"Model WHY segment (invented interview)","prompt":"Write a 90-second WHY segment that starts with purpose, includes a turning point and ends with a call to action.","steps":["Open with the physician’s belief, not their job title.","Describe the turning point with sensory detail.","Add one exact quote from the interview.","Show the impact on patients today.","Turn the story toward the audience with a call to action."],"answer":"“Dr. Ana Morales believes no child should grow up without a doctor who knows their name. When she was eleven, her little brother had an asthma attack at 2 a.m., and the nearest emergency room was 50 miles away. She remembers the sound of his breathing in the back seat. ‘That drive is why I came home,’ she told me. Today she cares for more than 2,000 people in that same county—including her brother’s children. Somewhere in a town like hers, a family is waiting for a doctor who knows their name. Maybe that doctor is in this room.”"},{"t":"check","q":"Why does the model segment open with the physician’s belief rather than her job title?","options":["Starting with why connects to emotion and purpose","Job titles are not allowed","It saves time on slides","Judges dislike job titles"],"why":"Start With Why: purpose first, then how and what."}]]
],takeaways:['Start With Why: what the physician does, how they do it and—at the center—why; inspiring stories start with why.','Use a story arc with a turning point, concrete detail, the physician’s own words and a call to action, told accurately and de-identified.','Objective (f) may conclude yes or no; support it with specific learning, strengths, concerns and a next step.'],terms:[['Golden Circle','Sinek’s model of why, how and what.'],['Story arc','Beginning, turning point, struggle and resolution.'],['Call to action','A closing request that moves the audience to act.']],mcqs:[q('In the Golden Circle, the center ring is:','Why','What','How','When','Inspiring communicators start with why.'),q('Which statement expresses a “why”?','“I believe every family deserves a doctor who knows them.”','“I see 20 patients a day.”','“I work in a clinic.”','“I use an electronic health record.”','Why is purpose or belief.'),q('Which rubric description is in the top band for the WHY story?','Evokes emotion, is highly impactful and encourages a call to action','Lists facts only','Is brief and neutral','Avoids emotion','Judges look for impact.'),q('Objective (f) may conclude that family medicine is:','Either a good fit or not a good fit','Only a good fit','Not relevant','The only career option','HOSA accepts either conclusion.'),q('How should patient stories be shared?','De-identified, without names or identifying details','With full names for credibility','With photos of patients','Not at all, even anonymously','Protect privacy.'),q('Which detail “shows” rather than “tells”?','“She keeps a drawing a patient made the day his asthma attacks stopped.”','“She is caring.”','“He is passionate.”','“They work hard.”','Concrete moments create emotion.'),q('The WHY story and fit reflection are worth together:','40 points','10 points','5 points','100 points','Each is worth 20.'),q('What does weaving facts into the story achieve?','Engagement and distinctiveness while covering all objectives','Fewer objectives covered','A shorter presentation','No need for research','Integration is memorable.')],frq:{prompt:'Using the Start With Why framework, outline how you would tell your interviewed family physician’s story and then explain whether family medicine is right for you.',rubric:[['Golden Circle applied',['why','how','what','purpose','golden circle']],['Story arc and detail',['turning point','moment','obstacle','quote','detail','call to action']],['Ethics',['accurate','permission','de-identif','privacy','exaggerat']],['Personal fit reflection',['strength','value','concern','learned','next step','fit']]],model:'I would open with the physician’s why: Dr. Patel believes no family in her rural town should have to travel two hours for care, because her own grandfather died after a delayed diagnosis. Then I would show the how—she builds long relationships, sees whole families and partners with the school nurse—and the what—she provides prenatal care, manages chronic disease and runs a weekly clinic at the senior center. The arc would move from her childhood in the town to the turning point of her grandfather’s illness, the obstacles of medical school debt and long hours in residency, and the resolution of returning home with NHSC loan repayment, using her exact quotes and only de-identified patient examples she approved. I would close with a call to action to consider rural primary care. For myself, I learned that I value continuity like the relationships I built tutoring younger students and that I enjoy variety more than focusing on one organ system; my concern is the length of training, but the NHSC scholarship makes it manageable, so my next step is shadowing a family physician this summer. Family medicine feels like a strong fit for me.'},sources:[RULES,SINEK,HIPAA]};

export const F9={title:'Creating the peer-to-peer presentation',hook:'Your first audience is your peers—classmates who may never have thought about family medicine. If you can hold their attention and change how they think about the career, you have built a presentation that will also impress judges. Creativity, visual aids and organization earn 50 points.',objectives:['Design a presentation that educates peers on all six objectives.','Use creative approaches that make the presentation distinctive.','Create visual aids that add value rather than substitute for speaking.','Deliver the peer presentation live and use feedback to improve.'],sections:[
['Requirements for the peer presentation',[
'The peer presentation—titled “Who is a Family Medicine Physician?” or another title of your choosing—is **educational and creative**, lasts **no more than ten minutes**, covers the **learning objectives** and tells the **story** of the interviewed physician and/or medical student. It must be delivered **live** to a peer audience: your HOSA chapter, a health science class, a school assembly or club meeting. It may be **virtual** (for example, on a video call) but must be **live**, not prerecorded. You may use any aids—slides, Prezi, video clips, photos, posters, handouts—but **you must speak**, using aids only to enhance your message. Record the **date and place** of the peer presentation; you will state it to judges.',
chk('Which peer presentation is allowed?',['A live video-call presentation to your HOSA chapter','Posting a prerecorded video online','Emailing slides to classmates','A recorded audio file played at an assembly'],'Virtual is acceptable only if delivered live.')]],
['Making it distinctive',[
'The **distinctive, captivating, unique** item is worth 15 points; the top band describes a presentation that is “highly creative, original and imaginative.” Ideas include structuring the talk as **a day in the life** of the physician hour by hour; framing it as a **patient’s journey** across the life span (newborn visit, sports physical, prenatal visit, diabetes care, end-of-life care); a **“choose your path” interactive** where the audience votes on decisions in the physician’s career; a **myth-busting** format (“Myth: family doctors only treat colds”); brief **live polls** or a short game; a meaningful **prop**, such as a stethoscope or the physician’s own white coat story; or a **two-voice** contrast between the physician’s and medical student’s journeys. Creativity should serve the content—every element should help peers understand family medicine.',
sort('Creative choice that serves the content?',['Serves the content','Distracts from the content'],[['A day-in-the-life timeline built around the physician’s schedule',0],['A myth-versus-fact segment on family medicine',0],['A long unrelated dance video',1],['An audience poll on how long a new-patient wait is',0],['Flashing animations on every slide',1],['A prop that represents the physician’s turning point',0]])]],
['Visual aids that add value',[
'Visual aids are worth **20 points**—as much as the WHY story. Judges reward aids that “add value and relevance to the presentation and are not used as substitutes” and that “help to tell a story.” Use **few words** per slide (a headline and a key phrase), large readable fonts, high-contrast colors and meaningful images—such as approved photos of the physician’s clinic (never patients without consent), a simple chart of demand data with its source, a timeline of training, or a map of shortage areas. Short video clips must be brief and purposeful, and you must still do most of the talking. Cite data sources on slides. Test everything on battery power, since **no electricity** is provided at the judged presentation, and prepare a backup in case technology fails.',
match('Match the content to an effective visual',[['Medical education path','Timeline graphic from college to board certification'],['Demand and outlook','Simple sourced chart of projected shortage'],['Health equity','Diagram of factors that shape health beyond clinical care'],['The physician’s story','Approved photo of the physician in their community'],['Career options','Icons for settings and fellowships'],['Finances','Comparison of loan repayment programs']])]],
['Organization: opening, flow and closing',[
'Organization earns 15 points across three items. The **opening** should **establish the occasion and purpose**, **grab attention** and make the audience want to listen—for example, a striking question (“Who here has a doctor who knows your whole family?”), a short story moment or a surprising fact. **Flow and transitions** should show evidence of practice: use signposts (“Now that you know how long the journey is, let’s talk about what life is like at the end of it”) and a logical sequence. The **closing** should prepare the audience for the end and finish **memorably**, often returning to the opening image or the physician’s words and ending with a call to action.',
order('A sample ten-minute structure',['Attention-grabbing opening and purpose','The physician’s story begins: who they are and their WHY','Demand and why now','The training path and career options','A week in the life: work, lifestyle and finances','Primary care, prevention and health equity in action','Is family medicine right for me?','Memorable close and call to action'])]],
['Delivering to peers and improving',[
'Present to your peers as if the stakes were high: rehearse, arrive early, test technology and engage the audience with eye contact and questions. Ask a teacher or friend to time you and take notes. Afterward, gather **feedback**—a short anonymous form asking what was most memorable, what was confusing and whether the audience learned something new about family medicine. Use it to sharpen the presentation before competition, but remember that the judged version should be **the same presentation**, so improvements should be refinements rather than a new talk.',
kase('Using peer feedback',{a:{text:'After your chapter presentation, several peers say the finances section was confusing because it had many numbers. What should you do?',choices:[['Simplify to two or three sourced figures and a clear visual comparison','b','Correct. Refine for clarity while keeping the same content.',true],['Remove finances entirely','b','Objective (c) is scored; refine it instead.',false]]},b:{text:'Peers loved the myth-busting game at the start. Should you keep it for judges?',choices:[['Yes—keep it, since the judged presentation should be the same and it is distinctive','c','Correct. It strengthens the opening and distinctiveness.',true],['No—judges dislike interactive openings','c','Engaging openings score well.',false]]},c:{text:'Your refined presentation keeps its creative opening and has a clearer finances section.',end:true}})]],
["Accessibility and audience engagement",["A presentation that everyone can follow is more effective. Use **large fonts** (at least about 24 points on slides), **high contrast** between text and background, and avoid relying on color alone to convey meaning. Add **captions** to video clips, describe key images aloud for anyone who cannot see them clearly, and keep animations minimal. Use plain language and define terms such as residency, primary care and social determinants the first time they appear. Choose inclusive images and examples that reflect diverse patients and physicians.","Engagement keeps peers listening. Ask a question and pause for hands, run a quick poll, invite the audience to predict a statistic before you reveal it, or use a short story pause (“What would you have done?”). Move purposefully, vary your voice and make eye contact around the room. Keep interactions brief so they support, rather than replace, the content, and plan how you will adapt them for three judges—for example, asking a rhetorical question instead of a show of hands.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Accessible or not?","buckets":["Accessible","Not accessible"],"items":[["Captions on a video clip",0],["Text that is only distinguished by red and green color",1],["Large, high-contrast slide text",0],["Undefined abbreviations such as FQHC and HPSA",1],["Describing a chart aloud",0],["Rapid flashing animations",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['The peer presentation is live (in person or virtual), at most ten minutes, educational and creative, covering all objectives and the physician’s story.','Distinctiveness (15) and visual aids (20) reward creative formats and aids that add value without replacing your voice.','Organization: an attention-grabbing opening with purpose, practiced transitions and a memorable closing with a call to action.'],terms:[['Visual aid','Image, slide, prop or media that supports a spoken message.'],['Signpost','A transition phrase that tells the audience where the talk is going.'],['Attention getter','An opening that captures interest.']],mcqs:[q('How long may the peer presentation be?','No more than 10 minutes','No more than 5 minutes','30 minutes','No limit','It matches the judged presentation.'),q('Which audience is acceptable for the peer presentation?','A HOSA chapter meeting','Only family members','No audience','A prerecorded YouTube audience','It must be live to peers.'),q('Visual aids are worth:','20 points','5 points','10 points','2 points','They help tell the story.'),q('What earns the top distinctiveness score?','A highly creative, original and imaginative presentation','A standard list of facts','Reading slides aloud','No visuals','Distinctiveness is worth 15.'),q('An effective opening should:','Establish the occasion and purpose and grab attention','Start with a list of references','Begin with an apology','Skip any introduction','Openings are scored.'),q('Which slide design is best?','A headline, a key phrase and a meaningful image','Full paragraphs in small font','Flashing animations on every word','No contrast between text and background','Less text is clearer.'),q('Can the peer presentation be virtual?','Yes, if delivered live','No, never','Only if prerecorded','Only as an email','Live delivery is required.'),q('Peer feedback should lead to:','Refinements to the same presentation','A completely new presentation for judges','Removing scored objectives','Ignoring all comments','The judged version should match.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe a creative ten-minute peer presentation on family medicine, including its format, how it covers each learning objective, the visual aids you would use and how you would open and close it.',rubric:[['Creative format',['day in the life','journey','myth','interactive','poll','story']],['Coverage of objectives',['demand','education','lifestyle','equity','why','right for me']],['Visual aids',['visual','slide','chart','timeline','image','source']],['Opening and closing',['opening','attention','purpose','closing','call to action','memorable']]],model:'My presentation would follow “24 Hours with Dr. Patel,” a rural family physician, as a clock moves across the screen. I would open by asking the audience to raise their hands if they have a doctor who knows their whole family, then state my purpose: to show who family physicians are and why we need more of them. At 7 a.m. Dr. Patel reviews her overbooked schedule, introducing a sourced chart of the primary care shortage (demand). At 9 a.m. a medical student shadows her, and a timeline shows the path from college to board certification and fellowship options (education). At noon she eats lunch while answering portal messages, leading to work-life balance, salary and loan repayment comparisons (lifestyle and finances). At 3 p.m. she connects a patient with a food pantry, illustrated by a diagram of social determinants (equity). At 6 p.m. she tells the story of her grandfather that inspired her (the WHY). I would end at midnight with my own reflection on whether family medicine fits me, then return to the opening question and close with a call to action to explore primary care.'},sources:[RULES,AAFP,SINEK]};

export const F10={title:'Presenting to the judges',hook:'Ten minutes, one table, no electricity and three judges who have heard many presentations. Starting with a clear verification statement, keeping to time and delivering with confidence turn your peer presentation into a winning performance.',objectives:['Open with the required verification statement.','Manage the ten-minute time limit and the one-minute warning.','Prepare battery-powered technology and backups for the competition room.','Deliver with strong voice, stage presence and diction.'],sections:[
['The verification statement',[
'To begin, the competitor states **who they interviewed**, **when the interviews took place** and **when and where the peer presentation was given**. HOSA’s example format: “I interviewed Dr. ___ at ___ on March 1. I interviewed ___, medical student at ___, on March 2. I presented this content to my HOSA chapter on April 3.” This statement earns the **5-point overview item** and is needed only in the judged version, not the peer presentation. It counts within the ten minutes, so practice saying it in about 20 seconds.',
chk('What must the verification statement include?',['The two interviewees’ names and dates, and the date and place of the peer presentation','Your GPA and school address','The interviewees’ salaries','Your HOSA membership number only'],'It confirms interviews and the peer presentation.')]],
['Time management',[
'The presentation has **ten total minutes**, including the verification statement. The timekeeper announces when **one minute remains** and **stops** the presentation at ten minutes. Plan a time budget—for example, 0:20 verification, 0:40 opening, about 6 minutes for objectives a–e woven into the story, 1:30 for “Is it right for me?” and 0:45 for the close—and rehearse with a timer at least five times. Mark a **cut point**: content you can skip if you fall behind, so the 20-point reflection and memorable closing are never lost.',
{t:'table',caption:'A sample judged time budget',head:['Segment','Time'],rows:[['Verification statement','0:00–0:20'],['Opening and purpose','0:20–1:00'],['Story and objectives a–e','1:00–7:15'],['Is family medicine right for me?','7:15–8:45'],['Close and call to action','8:45–9:30'],['Buffer','9:30–10:00']]},
kase('Running long',{a:{text:'The timekeeper announces one minute remaining, but you have not reached your reflection. What do you do?',choices:[['Skip to a 30-second version of your reflection and deliver your closing line','b','Correct. Protect the 20-point reflection and the closing.',true],['Keep presenting the current slide in full','b','You would be stopped before the reflection and closing.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you prevent this next time?',choices:[['Rehearse with a timer and mark cut points in the middle content','c','Correct. Planned cuts keep high-value items safe.',true],['Speak twice as fast','c','Rushing harms delivery scores.',false]]},c:{text:'In your next rehearsal, you finish at 9:35 with every objective covered.',end:true}})]],
['Logistics in the competition room',[
'Report to the event site at your appointment time in **business attire or the HOSA uniform** (dress bonus) with photo ID. HOSA provides **one table** only. There is **no electricity**—use a charged laptop or tablet, bring a battery pack and test that the device lasts well beyond ten minutes. **Internet is not provided**; if you need it, bring your own hotspot, but it is safer to store videos and slides **offline**. Props, posters and handouts are allowed if they support the presentation. Notecards are optional; electronic notecards must be on a **separate device** from the one judges see. Set up quickly and have a backup plan—printed visuals or a second file—if technology fails.',
sort('Provided by HOSA or the competitor?',['Provided by HOSA','Provided by the competitor'],[['One table',0],['Laptop or tablet',1],['Battery pack',1],['Wi-Fi hotspot if internet is needed',1],['Posters or props',1],['Electricity',1]])]],
['Delivery: voice, presence and diction',[
'Delivery earns 15 points. **Voice**: project so every judge hears you, vary rate and volume, and pause after key points and emotional moments in the story. **Stage presence**: stand tall, make eye contact with each judge, use purposeful gestures and let your enthusiasm show. **Diction, pronunciation and grammar**: choose precise words, pronounce terms correctly (residency, osteopathic, fellowship, determinants) and avoid fillers such as “um” and “like.” Speak to the judges as you spoke to your peers—conversationally and with conviction. Record a rehearsal to spot fillers and distracting habits.',
flip('Delivery fixes',[['Speaking too fast','Pause at every transition and after each statistic'],['Looking only at slides','Glance at the slide, then turn to the judges'],['Filler words','Replace “um” with a silent pause'],['Monotone story','Slow down and lower your volume at the emotional moment'],['Fidgeting','Plant your feet and use deliberate gestures'],['Reading notecards','Use cards only for key words and transitions']])]],
['The final checklist',[
'In the days before competition, confirm the Interview Verification Form was uploaded by May 15, finalize the presentation, charge all devices, download media for offline use and pack backups. Rehearse the full presentation, including the verification statement, under realistic conditions—standing, with your device on battery and a timer. Prepare your attire and photo ID. On the day, arrive early, breathe, smile and remember that you are telling a story you know better than anyone in the room.',
order('Competition-day sequence',['Arrive early in business attire with photo ID','Set up the device on the table and test it on battery','Greet the judges','Deliver the verification statement','Present the peer presentation, watching for the one-minute warning','Close memorably and thank the judges','Pack up quickly and leave the room'])]],
["Handling the unexpected",["Even well-prepared presentations face surprises. If your **device fails**, stay calm, try your backup quickly and continue with printed visuals or no visuals—the judges score your content and delivery, and composure itself shows stage presence. If you **forget a line**, pause, glance at your notecard and continue; audiences rarely notice a planned-looking pause. If you realize you **skipped a section**, add a brief transition later rather than going back awkwardly. If judges look neutral, do not assume the worst; many judges deliberately keep a neutral expression.","Follow HOSA’s General Rules and Regulations throughout: no outside help during the event, respectful conduct and adherence to time limits. After the presentation, thank the judges, collect your materials promptly and reflect on what went well and what you would improve. Whatever the result, the interviews, research and peer teaching have prepared you to discuss the career with confidence—an outcome that serves you in interviews, applications and future HOSA events.",{"t":"check","q":"Your laptop freezes two minutes into the judged presentation. What is the best response?","options":["Stay calm, try the backup briefly and continue with printed visuals or speaking alone","Stop and ask to restart the whole presentation later","Leave the room","Spend the remaining time fixing the laptop"],"why":"Composure and content still earn points."}]]
],takeaways:['Open with the verification statement: interviewees, interview dates and the date and place of the peer presentation (5 points).','Ten minutes total with a one-minute warning; budget time and mark cut points to protect the reflection and closing.','No electricity or internet: battery devices, offline media and backups; deliver with strong voice, presence and diction.'],terms:[['Verification statement','Opening statement confirming interviews and the peer presentation.'],['Cut point','Planned content to skip if running long.'],['Stage presence','Poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm.']],mcqs:[q('What should the competitor state at the start of the judged presentation?','Who they interviewed, when, and when and where the peer presentation occurred','Their final score','The judges’ names','The cost of their slides','This earns the overview points.'),q('How long is the judged presentation?','10 minutes total','10 minutes plus prep time','5 minutes','15 minutes','Prep time was removed.'),q('What does HOSA provide in the room?','One table','Electricity and Wi-Fi','A projector','A laptop','Bring everything else.'),q('When does the timekeeper give a warning?','When one minute remains','At five minutes','At the start','No warning is given','The presentation stops at ten minutes.'),q('If you need internet, you must:','Provide your own hotspot','Use the hotel Wi-Fi provided by HOSA','Ask the judges','Skip it','Internet is not provided.'),q('Which delivery habit improves voice scores?','Varying rate and volume and pausing','Speaking in a monotone','Whispering','Reading every word quickly','Vocal variety is scored.'),q('Is the verification statement required in the peer presentation?','No, only in the judged presentation','Yes, in both','Only in the peer version','Neither','It is for judges.'),q('Electronic notecards must be:','On a separate device from the one shown to judges','Projected for judges','Shared with the audience','On the presentation screen','Only aids may be shown.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would prepare for and deliver the ten-minute judged presentation, including the opening verification, time management, logistics and delivery techniques.',rubric:[['Verification statement',['interviewed','date','peer presentation','where','when']],['Time management',['10 minutes','one minute','budget','cut','timer','rehearse']],['Logistics',['battery','no electricity','hotspot','offline','table','backup']],['Delivery',['voice','eye contact','gesture','filler','diction','pause']]],model:'I would begin by stating, “I interviewed Dr. Maria Patel at Valley Family Health on March 1 and Jordan Kim, a medical student at State University, on March 3. I presented this content to my HOSA chapter on April 5,” which takes about 20 seconds. Then I would deliver the same presentation I gave my peers, using a time budget: 40 seconds for the opening, about six minutes for the story woven with objectives a–e, a minute and a half for whether family medicine is right for me and 45 seconds for the close, with a buffer. I would rehearse with a timer at least five times and mark cut points so that, after the one-minute warning, I could skip to my reflection and closing. Because HOSA provides only a table and no electricity or internet, I would bring a fully charged laptop and battery pack, store all media offline and carry printed backups. I would deliver with varied volume and pauses, steady eye contact with each judge, purposeful gestures and clear pronunciation without fillers, speaking conversationally and with conviction.'},sources:[RULES,GRR,UPLOAD]};
